//? Title: NSGA-II: ranks, crowding and finite survival
//? Subtitle: Actual dominance decisions, selected fronts and the original ZDT1 experiment
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Approximate multi-objective trade-offs with native nondominated sorting,
crowding-based survival and shared SBX/polynomial mutation. Actual canonical
histories explain the original six objective vectors and a finite-capacity
truncation control. The original30-variable ZDT1 run is retained with freshly
computed coverage and vertical-gap summaries, while actual tournaments expose
the selection mechanism without an unlimited-archive claim.
)

\toc

> Dominance creates fronts, not a single optimum

All objectives are minimized. A point dominates another when it is no worse in
every objective and strictly better in at least one. The first front contains
points with no dominator; removing that front exposes the next, and repeating
partitions the cohort. Native ranks are zero-based, so rank0 corresponds to the
traditional first front. Equal objective vectors do not dominate each other and
may remain as duplicate points.

The producer visits every unordered pair in original index order. It records
which point dominates, if either, and maintains domination counts/lists.
Decrementing counts after removing a front discovers the next front. This uses
O(mP²) objective-comparison work and O(P²) dominated-set storage in the worst
case for P points and m objectives. Actual counters count pair visits and shared
dominance calls, rather than inventing a platform-independent number of
component or CPU comparisons.

> Retain the original six objective vectors

The original A..F values are(.1,.9),(.3,.4),(.6,.2),(.9,.1),(.5,.6),(.8,.5). B
dominates E, while B and C dominate F; A through D form the first front. The
native ranking trace supplies the complete partition, actual pair relations and
actual crowding values. The diagram reads the actual two fronts, and the table
retains every point.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nsga_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/optim/nsga2.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::optim::nsga2_rank_trace(dm::optim::nsga2_demo_points());
    auto &r = t.ranking;
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < r.fronts.size(); ++i) {
        d.node(std::to_string(i), {9. * i, 0},
               std::format("Actual rank {} front\noriginal IDs {}", i,
                           r.fronts[i]));
        if (i)
            d.edge(std::to_string(i - 1), std::to_string(i),
                   "remove earlier front");
    }
    std::print("| original name | objective1 | objective2 | actual zero-based rank | actual crowding |\n| " "--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < r.points.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", char('A' + i),
                   r.points[i].objectives[0], r.points[i].objectives[1],
                   r.ranks[i], r.crowding[i]);
    std::print("\n| actual dominating ID | actual dominated ID |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &pair : t.pairs)
        if (pair.relation)
            std::print("| {} | {} |\n",
                       pair.relation < 0 ? pair.first : pair.second,
                       pair.relation < 0 ? pair.second : pair.first);
    d.save_svg("build/nsga_tiny.svg");
}
```
