> Contracts, complete costs and references

Complete finite strict bound vectors, finite widths and valid rate/index options
are checked before any callback. Requested populations zero/one become two; odd
values round up, with an effective population cap1000000. The first actual
callback must return2..1000000 finite objectives and every later callback must
retain that shape. Nonfinite/out-of-box variation coordinates, nonfinite
crowding ranges/gaps or changing callback outputs fail explicitly. Earlier
callback effects remain on later failure; no partial result/history is returned.

For G generations,N parents,m objectives and n variables, sorting costs
O((G+1)mN²), variation adds its actual work and crowding adds objective sorts;
final nondominated extraction is also quadratic. Dominated-set storage is O(N²),
with population values O(N(n+m)). Selected work counters exclude callback cost,
standard-sort comparator counts and the final filter; they are not a hardware
instruction census.

Detailed traces own original bounds/options, point rows and values,
ranks/crowding/assigned entries, front/order IDs,
pair/tournament/parent/generation records, selected IDs and final
population/front records. Their logical quota defaults to1000000 and zero
disables; admission precedes retained records/copies. Complete pair histories
can be quadratic per generation, so a large ordinary run need not fit a detailed
trace’s default budget. This is a logical-history policy, not a peak-RAM claim.

[Deb, Pratap, Agarwal and Meyarivan (2002), A fast and elitist multiobjective genetic algorithm:NSGA-II|https://doi.org/10.1109/4235.996017]
describes nondominated sorting and crowding survival.
[Zitzler, Deb and Thiele (2000), Comparison of multiobjective evolutionary algorithms: empirical results|https://doi.org/10.1162/106365600568202]
provides the ZDT benchmark context. The explicit truncation control and actual
histories distinguish elitist finite survival from an unlimited Pareto archive
or guaranteed convergence.

